task: throws-into
agent: fable51-core
started: 2026-09-11
branch: feature/throws-into
worktree: ../okay-wt-throws-into
spec: docs/typepedia.md (the `throws` entry) and docs/direct-style.md,
  which today tells the reader the language import is "the language
  demanding consent"

why: operator, 2026-09-11 — Scala 3.9's `into` modifier, then "бери"
  on the one place I found for it. `into` marks the TARGET type of a
  conversion and may be written only on a class, a trait or an opaque
  type alias. `throws` is an opaque infix type whose whole design is
  absorbing four shapes, and four `Conversion` givens into it already
  exist — so today every user of `A throws E` must write
  `import scala.language.implicitConversions`.

what:
  1. `into opaque infix type throws[+A, +E <: Unsafe]`.
  2. DELETE the fifth conversion, `Conversion[A throws E,
     Either[E | Unsafe, A]]`. It is the one that keeps the import
     alive — its target is `Either`, not `throws`, so `into` cannot
     reach it — and it is redundant: its body is literally `_.wrap`,
     and `.wrap`/`??` are public and used explicitly 38 times.
  3. drop the now-unneeded import from the sites that had it for
     `throws` (TestThrows.scala is the only one in-repo; the other
     four are Direct's, which `into` cannot help).
  4. doc: the `throws` entry in typepedia, and docs/direct-style.md's
     line about consent, which stays true for Direct and stops being
     true for throws.

measured before claiming (scratchpad probes, scala-cli 3.9.0):
  - `into` on the REAL declaration, bounds and all, compiles and all
    four absorbing conversions fire with NO language import.
  - with the fifth conversion's use present: 1 feature warning;
    with it removed: zero. That is what pins the warning to it.
  - `into enum` and `into trait` also compile — checked because the
    Json case looked tempting at ~850 construction sites, and was
    REFUSED: `Conversion[String, Json]` turns an already-serialized
    JSON string into a JSON string literal silently, which is double
    encoding in the module whose job is encoding.
  - Direct's `Conversion[F[A], A]` cannot be helped at all: the
    target is a bare type variable and `into` marks a declaration.
    Four of the repository's five language imports are Direct's and
    they stay.

predict: nothing in the repository relies on the fifth conversion
  implicitly — `catching` already calls `.wrap` by hand. If the
  compile says otherwise, those sites get an explicit `.wrap` and the
  conversion still goes. Recorded so being wrong shows.

CAUTION: deleting a public conversion is a source-breaking change for
  a user who leaned on it. It is redundant rather than lost, and the
  CHANGELOG entry will say so in those words.
